.TH KBD 3
.SH NAME
scancode \- the raw keyboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #c /dev
.nf

.B /dev/scancode
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Scancode
is the keyboard's stream before translation: the bytes the keyboard driver
would otherwise turn into console input, unread.
It is one of the files the
.B #c
device serves.
See
.IR cons (3)
for the rest of the directory.
.PP
While nothing has
.B scancode
open, the kernel's own keyboard driver reads every scancode and turns it
into characters on
.BR /dev/cons .
The first open of
.B scancode
diverts the stream: from that point the driver sees nothing, and every
scancode goes to this file instead, until the last fid holding it closes.
A copy of the stream was rejected in favour of this: two things turning
scancodes into characters at once would act on every keystroke twice.
Bytes typed while nothing holds
.B scancode
belong to the console, and bytes typed while it is diverted belong to this
file: neither queues for the other, and the ring is drained both when a fid
opens the file and when the last one closes it.
When the last fid closes, the keyboard driver resets its own idea of which
modifier keys are down, so a shift released while the file was diverted
does not leave the console stuck as if it were still held.
.PP
A read parks until a scancode arrives, unless one is already waiting.
Nothing here is a line discipline: every byte that arrives answers a read
exactly as it came off the keyboard, none of it means anything to this
file, and none of it is echoed.
There is no end of file: a read answers a scancode or it parks, and it
never answers zero bytes.
.B Scancode
may be opened more than once at a time.
Every open reads the same stream, and a scancode goes to whichever open
read comes first.
.PP
.B Scancode
is read-only.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/devfs/tap.odin
.br
.B kernel/drivers/kbd
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR cons (3),
.IR uart (3),
.IR kbdfs (4),
.IR kbd (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A write to
.B scancode
fails with
.BR EPERM .
A read that is still unanswered when the connection abandons it fails with
.BR EINTR .
